+## Clients, projects, and named partners
+
+LeJeune's own portfolio (`/portfolio-items/`) is the primary public job list. Most pages do **not** name the fabricator, erector, or GC. Where a third party is named, it is quoted below. Jon Caven's public LinkedIn project block adds four later jobs that are **not** on the company portfolio; treat those as employee-published, not as a company press release.
+
+### Packet jobs
+
+**U.S. Bank Stadium (Minnesota Vikings), Minneapolis.** Company portfolio: "We are proud to have supplied and supported the steel construction and erection of this new local icon." Finished 2016. Vikings TCO Performance Center (Eagan) page: "after our success in supplying the iconic U.S. Bank Stadium our dedicated customer chose LeJeune Bolt to supply this equally impressive project." The customer is unnamed. **Do not merge this with LeJeune Steel.** Peddinghaus and LeJeune Steel's own materials document LeJeune Steel as fabricator of U.S. Bank Stadium trusses. Both firms can be on the same job. ([U.S. Bank Stadium](https://lejeunebolt.com/portfolio-items/us-bank-stadium/); [Vikings TCO](https://lejeunebolt.com/portfolio-items/vikings-tco-performance-center/); [Peddinghaus / LeJeune Steel](https://www.peddinghaus.com/lejeune-steel-scores-big-with-the-minnesota-vikings-stadium/videos/64))
+
+**NASA / Mobile Launcher 2.** Public evidence is TNA on NASA's ML2 **ground platform** for Artemis, not a separate line of NASA-proprietary spaceflight fasteners. STRUCTURE magazine sponsored post (2025-07-01): "The TNA Torque+Angle Fastening System was utilized throughout the build of NASA's new Mobile Launcher 2." YouTube NCSEA 2025 description: "used by NASA on the new Mobile Launcher 2." Jon Caven LinkedIn: "NASA and Bechtel are constructing the ML 2 launch pad for the rocket to Mars. They elected to use our ASTM F3148 fastening system." The packet's "proprietary, patented fasteners" language is **PACKET / UNVERIFIED** as a NASA-owned fastener family. The patents on the public record are Chad M. Larson's torque-angle system (see Products). ([STRUCTURE sponsored post](https://www.structuremag.org/article/sponsored-post-this-bolt-launches-spaceships/); [YouTube NCSEA 2025](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jYv677bn7Mg); Jon Caven LinkedIn (LinkedIn individual profile — URL withheld, public-repo hygiene))
+
+**Mystic Lake Amphitheater, Shakopee MN.** Packet (CAPTURE.md plus photos) says LeJeune Bolt sold bolts for the structure around the stage (Rufus Du Sol). Public construction credits found: owner/contractor Swervo Development, architect Shea Inc., structural engineer ERA Structural Engineering, other partners **LeJeune Steel Company** and Danny's Construction. Star Tribune and Wikipedia confirm Live Nation operator, ~19,000 capacity, opened June 2026. **No public page found that names LeJeune Bolt as fastener supplier.** Possible name collision with LeJeune Steel. Status: **PACKET / UNVERIFIED on the public web.** ([ERA Facebook, 2026-05-12](https://www.facebook.com/erastructural/posts/congratulations-to-the-entire-project-team-behind-the-mystic-lake-amphitheater-o/1638653270706240/); [Star Tribune, 2026-05-11](https://www.startribune.com/sneak-peak-mystic-lake-amphitheater-shakopee-live-nation-opening-construction-prices-tickets/601806696); [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mystic_Lake_Amphitheater); [CAPTURE.md](../CAPTURE.md))
